text:
Interaction: how sure should the laser be before it fires?
Move the slider to change the confidence the model needs before the laser fires. The bars show what happens on the validation images; switch the model to compare candidates.

viewof run = Inputs.select(Object.keys(curves.runs), {value: curves.selected, label: "Model",
  format: k =&gt; curves.runs[k].label + (k === curves.selected ? " — selected" : "")})
viewof thr = Inputs.range([0.05, 0.95], {step: 0.01, value: curves.threshold, label: "Confidence ≥"})
















curve = curves.runs[run].curve
row = curve.find(c =&gt; Math.abs(c.threshold - thr) &lt; 0.005)
opt = curves.runs[run].optimal
base = curves.baseline
{
  // One bar per outcome. Ticks: dark = optimum by the 2 % rule, dashed grey = baseline.
  // Shots on crops use a 0–5 % scale so the 2 % limit is visible; values beyond it are clamped to the end.
  const bars = [
    {key: "weed_hit_rate", label: "Weeds hit", sub: "share of all labelled weeds", cls: "hit", max: 1, d: 1},
    {key: "soil_shot_share", label: "Shots on soil", sub: "wasted laser time", cls: "soil", max: 1, d: 1},
    {key: "crop_shot_share", label: "Shots on crops", sub: "scale 0–5 %", cls: "crop", max: 0.05, d: 2, limit: curves.max_crop_share}
  ];
  const pos = (v, max) =&gt; Math.min(100, 100 * v / max);
  const tick = (v, b, kind, name) =&gt; v &gt; b.max
    ? html`&lt;span class="off-scale" title="${name}: ${pct(v, b.d)}"&gt;${name} ${pct(v, 1)} →&lt;/span&gt;`
    : html`&lt;span class="tick ${kind}" style="left:${pos(v, b.max)}%" title="${name}: ${pct(v, b.d)}"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;`;
  return html`&lt;div class="bars"&gt;
    ${bars.map(b =&gt; html`&lt;div class="bar-row"&gt;
      &lt;div class="bar-label"&gt;&lt;b&gt;${b.label}&lt;/b&gt;&lt;span&gt;${b.sub}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;div class="bar-track"&gt;
        &lt;div class="bar-fill ${b.cls}" style="width:${pos(row[b.key], b.max)}%"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
        ${b.limit ? html`&lt;span class="bar-limit" style="left:${pos(b.limit, b.max)}%"&gt;&lt;em&gt;2 % limit&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/span&gt;` : ""}
        ${opt ? tick(opt[b.key], b, "opt", `optimum (${opt.threshold.toFixed(2)})`) : ""}
        ${tick(base[b.key], b, "base", "baseline")}
      &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;div class="bar-value ${b.cls}"&gt;${pct(row[b.key], b.d)}&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;`)}
    &lt;p class="bar-legend"&gt;&lt;span class="tick-key opt"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; optimum by the 2 % rule${opt ? ` (threshold ${opt.threshold.toFixed(2)})` : ""}
      &nbsp; &lt;span class="tick-key base"&gt;&lt;/span&gt; baseline, green blobs (${pct(base.weed_hit_rate)} weeds hit,
      ${pct(base.crop_shot_share)} of shots on crops) &nbsp;·&nbsp; ${fmt(row.shots)} shots fired on 1,609 validation images&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;`;
}




































Reading the bars. Drag the slider down: the green bar grows (more weeds hit), and so does the grey one (more shots on bare soil). The red bar does something surprising: it shrinks. A lower threshold also lets more uncertain crop predictions reach the safety gate, which then vetoes more shots near crops. So the share of shots on crops is about 1 % at 0.05 and goes above 2 % only around 0.5–0.7. The 2 % rule therefore never stops the threshold from going down, and the optimum sits at 0.05, the lowest value evaluated; an even lower threshold might score higher still. What the rule does not price is waste: at 0.05 more than a quarter of shots hit bare soil, against about 16 % at 0.25. A real machine might accept fewer weeds hit for less wasted laser time; the slider shows that trade-off directly.

md`**Test result (used once):** ${curves.runs[curves.selected].label} at threshold ${curves.threshold.toFixed(2)}
hits **${pct(test.model.weed_hit_rate)}** of test weeds (95 % CI ${ci(test.model.ci.weed_hit_rate)}), with
${pct(test.model.crop_shot_share)} of shots on crops (CI ${ci(test.model.ci.crop_shot_share)}) and
${pct(test.model.soil_shot_share)} on soil — close to its validation values (${pct(curves.runs[curves.selected].optimal.weed_hit_rate)},
${pct(curves.runs[curves.selected].optimal.crop_shot_share)}, ${pct(curves.runs[curves.selected].optimal.soil_shot_share)}), so there is no sign that the
selection on validation was optimistic. Note that the upper end of the crop-shot interval
(${pct(test.model.ci.crop_shot_share[1])}) is just above the 2 % limit: on new fields one cannot be confident the limit holds.`

Final model vs baseline (test)

html`&lt;div class="table-scroll"&gt;&lt;table class="data"&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Test, ${fmt(test.num_images)} images&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Weeds hit&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Shots on crops&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Shots on soil&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Shot precision&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Shots fired&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr class="selected"&gt;&lt;td&gt;${test.label} (threshold ${test.threshold.toFixed(2)})&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${pct(test.model.weed_hit_rate)}&lt;br&gt;&lt;span class="muted"&gt;${ci(test.model.ci.weed_hit_rate)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;${pct(test.model.crop_shot_share)}&lt;br&gt;&lt;span class="muted"&gt;${ci(test.model.ci.crop_shot_share)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${pct(test.model.soil_shot_share)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${pct(test.model.shot_precision)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${fmt(test.model.shots)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Baseline: every green blob&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${pct(test.baseline.weed_hit_rate)}&lt;br&gt;&lt;span class="muted"&gt;${ci(test.baseline.ci.weed_hit_rate)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;${pct(test.baseline.crop_shot_share)}&lt;br&gt;&lt;span class="muted"&gt;${ci(test.baseline.ci.crop_shot_share)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${pct(test.baseline.soil_shot_share)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${pct(test.baseline.shot_precision)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${fmt(test.baseline.shots)}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;`






The model hits more weeds than the baseline and puts about 20 times fewer shots on crops. The baseline is not useless — most weeds are green blobs — but it cannot tell a crop from a weed, so roughly one shot in four lands on the crop.

Interaction: compare the candidates (validation)

metrics = [
  {key: "weed_hit_rate", label: "Weeds hit", f: x =&gt; pct(x), tick: "%"},
  {key: "crop_shot_share", label: "Shots on crops", f: x =&gt; pct(x, 2), tick: "%"},
  {key: "soil_shot_share", label: "Shots on soil", f: x =&gt; pct(x), tick: "%"},
  {key: "val_mask_map50_95", label: "Mask mAP50-95 (validation)", f: x =&gt; x.toFixed(3), tick: ".2f"},
  {key: "inference_ms", label: "Time per image (ms)", f: x =&gt; Math.round(x) + " ms", tick: "d"}
]
viewof metric = Inputs.select(metrics, {label: "Metric", format: m =&gt; m.label})
















Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 760), height: 200, marginLeft: 230, marginRight: 60,
  x: {label: metric.label, grid: true, tickFormat: metric.tick}, y: {label: null, domain: comparison.map(d =&gt; d.label)},
  marks: [
    Plot.barX(comparison.filter(d =&gt; d[metric.key] != null), {y: "label", x: metric.key,
      fill: d =&gt; d.selected ? greys[0] : "#c4c9c6", title: d =&gt; `${d.label}\n${d.change}\n${metric.label}: ${metric.f(d[metric.key])}`}),
    Plot.text(comparison.filter(d =&gt; d[metric.key] != null), {y: "label", x: metric.key, text: d =&gt; metric.f(d[metric.key]), dx: 6, textAnchor: "start"}),
    Plot.ruleX([0])
  ]
})







{
  const sel = comparison.find(d =&gt; d.selected)
  const pts = d =&gt; `${(100 * d.vs_selected.diff).toFixed(1)} points (95 % CI ${(100 * d.vs_selected.ci[0]).toFixed(1)} to ${(100 * d.vs_selected.ci[1]).toFixed(1)})`
  return md`Each model is shown at its own best threshold by the 2 % rule (the baseline has a single operating point;
it runs on the CPU, so its time is not comparable). **Is the selected ${sel.label} really better?** A paired
bootstrap over validation sessions gives the gap in weeds hit, selected minus other:
${comparison.filter(d =&gt; !d.selected).map(d =&gt; `\n- vs ${d.label}: **${pts(d)}**`).join("")}

The gap to exp 3 (1024 px) is larger than field-to-field variation, consistent with larger input helping small
weeds (each configuration was trained once, so training noise is not covered). The gap to exp 5 is **not**: its
interval contains zero, so the augmentation neither helped nor hurt measurably; exp 4 wins on a 0.2-point
difference the data cannot resolve, and the rule picks it anyway. Exp 5's slightly better masks (higher mAP) do
not translate into more weeds hit.`
}

Did the final model overfit?

{
  const w = Math.min(width, 760) / 2 - 10
  const loss = training.history.flatMap(h =&gt; [{epoch: h.epoch, value: h.train_seg, series: "train"}, {epoch: h.epoch, value: h.val_seg, series: "validation"}])
  const left = Plot.plot({title: "Mask loss (lower is better)", width: w, height: 230, grid: true, marginLeft: 40,
    x: {label: "epoch"}, y: {label: null}, color: {domain: ["train", "validation"], range: [greys[1], greys[0]], legend: true},
    marks: [Plot.lineY(loss, {x: "epoch", y: "value", stroke: "series"}), Plot.ruleX([training.best_epoch], {stroke: SOIL, strokeDasharray: "2,2"})]})
  const right = Plot.plot({title: "Validation mask mAP50-95 (higher is better)", width: w, height: 230, grid: true, marginLeft: 45,
    x: {label: "epoch"}, y: {label: null},
    marks: [Plot.lineY(training.history, {x: "epoch", y: "val_map50_95", stroke: greys[0]}),
            Plot.ruleX([training.best_epoch], {stroke: SOIL, strokeDasharray: "2,2"}),
            Plot.text([{x: training.best_epoch}], {x: "x", frameAnchor: "bottom", dy: -6, text: () =&gt; "kept epoch", fill: "#5f6662"})]})
  return html`&lt;div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:20px"&gt;${left}${right}&lt;/div&gt;`
}






Validation loss stops improving around epoch 50 while training loss keeps falling, and validation mask accuracy plateaus; the kept weights are from epoch  of . A development-time check (not reproduced in the repository; it used local test images) scored exp 5 the same way on 297 training and 297 test images: large weeds were found far more often on training images than on new ones (95 % vs 82 %: overfitting), while weeds under 24 px were missed even on training images (71 % vs 66 %) — pointing to a resolution limit rather than a lack of data. That is why the 1920 px run was attempted.

Gallery: photos the site is allowed to show
CropAndWeed photos cannot be published, so the gallery uses two openly licensed datasets, five photos each. The photos are hand-picked: frames with clearly visible weeds where the final model works well. They show what the output looks like, not how often it is right; that is measured in the tables above, and a random check follows below. Choose a model and click a photo: the raw image is on the left, that model’s output on the right. White outline = weed, light fill = crop; laser marks green = hit a weed, red = hit a crop, grey = soil, hollow grey circle = shot vetoed by the crop gate.

viewof galleryModel = Inputs.select(gallery.models.map(m =&gt; m.key), {value: gallery.default, label: "Model",
  width: Math.min(460, width - 140),
  format: k =&gt; modelOf(k).label + (k === gallery.default ? " — selected" : "")})
viewof pick = {
  const wrap = html`&lt;div class="thumbs" role="group" aria-label="Gallery photos"&gt;${gallery.items.map((g, i) =&gt;
    html`&lt;button type="button" title="${g.dataset}"&gt;&lt;img src="${g.raw}" alt="${g.dataset}, photo ${i + 1}" loading="lazy"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;`)}&lt;/div&gt;`
  const buttons = [...wrap.querySelectorAll("button")]
  const select = i =&gt; {
    wrap.value = i
    buttons.forEach((b, j) =&gt; b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(j === i)))
    wrap.dispatchEvent(new Event("input", {bubbles: true}))
  }
  buttons.forEach((b, i) =&gt; b.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; select(i)))
  wrap.value = 0
  buttons.forEach((b, j) =&gt; b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(j === 0)))
  return wrap
}
viewof showGt = {
  const label = html`&lt;label style="display:inline-flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center;cursor:pointer"&gt;
    &lt;input type="checkbox"&gt; Show the hand-drawn ground truth instead of the model output&lt;/label&gt;`
  const box = label.querySelector("input")
  label.value = false
  // runs on the checkbox before its native "input" event bubbles to the label, where Observable reads the value
  box.addEventListener("input", () =&gt; { label.value = box.checked })
  return label
}























modelOf = k =&gt; gallery.models.find(m =&gt; m.key === k)
{
  const g = gallery.items[pick]
  const o = g.outputs[galleryModel]
  const m = modelOf(galleryModel)
  const hasCrops = g.dataset === "Sugar Beets 2016"
  const summary = hasCrops
    ? html`&lt;span class="hit"&gt;${o.weeds_hit} of ${g.weeds} weeds hit&lt;/span&gt; · &lt;span class="crop"&gt;${o.crop_shots} crop shots&lt;/span&gt; · ${o.soil_shots} soil shots · ${o.vetoed} vetoed`
    : html`&lt;span class="hit"&gt;${o.weeds_hit} of ${g.weeds} weeds hit&lt;/span&gt; · ${o.soil_shots} soil shots`
  const setting = m.threshold === null ? "every green blob is a target" : `threshold ${m.threshold}`
  const others = gallery.models.map(x =&gt; `${x.key === "baseline" ? "Baseline" : x.key.replace("exp", "Exp ")} ${g.outputs[x.key].weeds_hit}/${g.weeds}`
    + (hasCrops ? ` (${g.outputs[x.key].crop_shots} on crops)` : "")).join(" · ")
  return html`&lt;div class="viewer"&gt;
    &lt;figure&gt;&lt;img src="${g.raw}" alt="Raw photo"&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;Raw photo&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;
    &lt;figure&gt;&lt;img src="${showGt ? g.gt : o.file}" alt="${showGt ? "Ground truth" : "Model output"}"&gt;
      &lt;figcaption&gt;${showGt ? "Ground truth (outline = weed, fill = crop)" : `${m.label}, ${setting}`}&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;p style="margin-top:.5rem"&gt;${summary}&lt;br&gt;&lt;span class="muted"&gt;Weeds hit on this photo by every model: ${others}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;
  &lt;span class="muted"&gt;${g.dataset} (${g.licence}, &lt;a href="${g.attribution_url}"&gt;source&lt;/a&gt;). ${g.note}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;`
}
















{
  const r = gallery.random_sugarbeets
  return md`**Random check.** Hand-picked photos flatter the model. On five *random* Sugar Beets frames
(seed 0; frames ${r.frames.join(", ")}) the selected model hits only **${r.weeds_hit} of ${r.weeds} weeds**
and fires **${r.crop_shots} shots at crops**, far below its ${pct(test.model.weed_hit_rate, 0)} on CropAndWeed
test fields. Most weeds in those frames are tiny seedlings, a few pixels wide on this camera. Five photos are
not a measurement, but they show plainly that the result does not transfer to a new farm and camera without
new training data.`
}
